{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Fin.VecNotation","lean:module:Mathlib.GroupTheory.Perm.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.GroupTheory.SpecificGroups.Alternating","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Homology.ShortComplex.Exact","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.twist_surj_of_correction_trivial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.twist_surj_of_correction_trivial","kind":"theorem","line":283,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L283-L298","doc":"**Vanishing correction ⇒ the twist map is surjective.**\n\nStated for an exact sequence of additive groups: if `g ∘ f = 0` describes\nthe composite and every element of `B` killed by `g` is in the range of\n`f`, then a trivial `C` forces `f` to be onto.\n\nThe abstract shell of \"the twist is an isomorphism when the term supported\non the sphere vanishes\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.g2_dim_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.g2_dim_split","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L240-L252","doc":"**`dim SU(3) + dim SO(4) = dim G₂`.**\n\n`8 + 6 = 14`. Arithmetic, recorded for what it means: `SU(3)` (stabiliser\nof a unit imaginary octonion, quotient `G₂/SU(3) = S⁶`) and\n`SO(4) = (SU(2)×SU(2))/ℤ₂` (stabiliser of a quaternionic subalgebra\n`ℍ ⊂ 𝕆`, quotient the 8-dimensional quaternionic Wolf space `G₂/SO(4)`)\nare **complementary** inside `G₂`.\n\nThe tetraplectic `SU(2)` enters `G₂` through that `SO(4)`. So the two\nstructures the framework juggles are the two subgroups giving `G₂`'s two\nhomogeneous spaces — and `G₂`, not `SU(3)`, is where they meet. The corpus\nrecords `S⁶ = G₂/SU(3)` and says nothing anywhere about `G₂/SO(4)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.not_mulEquiv_perm5_of_center","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.not_mulEquiv_perm5_of_center","kind":"theorem","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L222-L236","doc":"**The order-120 trap, closed.** No group with a non-trivial central element\nis isomorphic to `S₅`.\n\n`2I`, the binary icosahedral group, is a central extension\n`1 → ℤ₂ → 2I → A₅ → 1`, so it has a non-trivial central element and this\napplies: **`2I ≇ S₅`**, despite `|2I| = |S₅| = 120 = |Aut(Bring)|`.\n\nThe one input still supplied in prose is `Z(2I) ≠ 1`, because `2I` is not in\nMathlib. Everything else is proved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.center_perm5_eq_bot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.center_perm5_eq_bot","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L187-L220","doc":"**`Z(S₅) = 1`** — the symmetric group on five letters has trivial centre.\n\nDerived here rather than cited: Mathlib does not ship it (checked). The\nargument is the standard one. If `σ` is central and `σ a = b ≠ a`, pick\n`c ∉ {a, b}` — `Fin 5` has room — and commute `σ` past the transposition\n`(b c)`. Evaluating both sides at `a` gives `c = b`, since the\ntransposition fixes `a` and sends `b` to `c`. Contradiction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.not_isomorphic_of_center","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.not_isomorphic_of_center","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L158-L184","doc":"**Equal order does not give isomorphism** — the centre separates them.\n\n`|Aut(Bring)| = |S₅| = 120 = |2I|`, the binary icosahedral group. But\n`Z(2I) = ℤ₂` (it is a central extension of `A₅`) while `Z(S₅) = 1`. So\n`2I ≇ S₅`, and an argument that reaches `2I ⊂ SU(2)` from \"Bring has\norder-120 symmetry\" has proved nothing.\n\nThe valid route is `A₅ ⊂ S₅`, which lifts to `2I ⊂ SU(2)`; and separately\n`A₅ ⊂ SO(3) ⊂ SU(3)` with **no** central extension needed — which is the\nasymmetry the whole SU(2)-vs-SU(3) question turns on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.blockEmbed_not_normal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.blockEmbed_not_normal","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L148-L154","doc":"The conjugate is outside the block, so the block-embedded `SU(2)` is not\nnormal in `SU(3)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.conj_leaves_block","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.conj_leaves_block","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L135-L146","doc":"**`SU(2)` is not normal in `SU(3)`.** Conjugating the block element\n`h = diag(i, -i, 1)` by the `3`-cycle moves `-i` into the `(2,2)` slot,\nwhich `InBlock` requires to be `1`.\n\nConsequence, and the reason this is here: there is **no** short exact\nsequence `1 → SU(2) → SU(3) → Q → 1`. Any programme phrased as \"lift the\ntetraplectic `SU(2)` to colour `SU(3)`\" is not an extension problem, and\ncannot be solved by finding the right quotient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.cyc3_orthogonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.cyc3_orthogonal","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L126-L133","doc":"`cyc3` is orthogonal, so its transpose is its inverse. Together with\n`det = +1` (real orthogonal `3`-cycle) this places it in `SU(3)`, and lets\nthe conjugation below be written without `⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.hWitness_inBlock","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.hWitness_inBlock","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L122-L124","doc":"The witness is in the block. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.InBlock","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.InBlock","kind":"def","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L117-L120","doc":"Membership in the block-embedded `SU(2)`: the third row and column are\nthose of the identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.hWitness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.hWitness","kind":"def","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L113-L115","doc":"The witness `h = diag(i, -i, 1)`, inside the top-left `2 × 2` block. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.cyc3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.cyc3","kind":"def","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L109-L111","doc":"The `3`-cycle permutation matrix: `e₀ ↦ e₁ ↦ e₂ ↦ e₀`. Real, orthogonal,\ndeterminant `+1`, so it lies in `SU(3)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.real_rep_selfConjugate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.StandardModel.ColourObstruction.real_rep_selfConjugate","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/ColourObstruction.lean#L79-L97","doc":"**A real matrix intertwines a representation with its conjugate.**\n\nIf every entry of `M` is real then conjugating the output of `M.mulVec`\nis the same as applying `M` to the conjugated input. So the representation\ngenerated by `M` and its complex conjugate are *the same matrix\nrepresentation*, hence isomorphic: `3 ≅ 3̄`.\n\nThis is why the three Reeb fields cannot be the colour triplet. Under the\nprincipal embedding `SU(2) ↪ SU(3)` the fundamental **3** restricts to the\nadjoint of `su(2)` — real matrices, the rotation action on `ℝ³ ⊂ ℂ³` —\nso `3 ≅ 3̄`, and there is no colour/anticolour distinction to be had.\nColour needs `3 ≇ 3̄`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}